  "response": "When P\u00e8re Armand David encountered a giant panda specimen in Sichuan, China, in 1869, his find led directly to the animal's original description under the bear name Ursus melanoleucus. Subsequently, Alphonse Milne-Edwards reassigned the giant panda to a position close to the red panda. That reassignment was brought about by the lesser animal's own address in the system, for the red panda is currently classified in the family Procyonidae with the raccoons.\n\nThe question thereafter became an empirical one, to be settled by characters less malleable than external resemblance, and cytogenetics supplied the first hard numbers. The giant panda has 42 chromosomes, whereas the red panda has 36 chromosomes; the panda's complement of 42 likewise stands in sharp contrast with the condition in most bear species, which have 74 chromosomes. Counts of this kind could narrow the field of possibilities, but they could not by themselves fix a family assignment, since chromosome number is notoriously labile across carnivore lineages.\n\nMolecular comparison proved more decisive. Vincent Sarich's 1973 serum immunology work aligned the giant panda with the bears; his serological verdict was, in other words, the very claim that the animal belongs inside the family Ursidae, and it came a dozen years before the multi-method reassessment of the problem. The 1985 investigation combined DNA hybridization, isozyme electrophoresis, immunological distance, and chromosomal banding. Mitochondrial DNA sequence comparisons placed the giant panda within the family Ursidae. Among living bears, the giant panda represents the earliest-diverging lineage.\n\nThe 2010 genome analysis showed that the giant panda's closest living relatives are the raccoons. This result and the panda's standing as the first branch to separate within the bear family cannot both be true, because a lineage seated inside Ursidae cannot have its nearest living kin outside that family altogether. The taxonomic question therefore reduces to a strict either/or: either the giant panda and the red panda belong to different families, or the giant panda and the red panda belong to the same family, and exactly one of those two situations obtains, never both. The long history of the dispute is in effect the history of deciding which of those two arms to accept, and the weight of laboratory comparison, rather than of impression and anatomy, is what carried the decision.",
